  "text": "Question eleven. What's the difference between a forest's built-in feature importance and SHAP values, short for Shapley additive explanations? The built-in one adds up how much each feature cut impurity, across every tree. It's fast, but it favors features with many distinct values. Here's a made-up churn table, with a customer I D that means nothing at all. Impurity importance gives the I D forty percent, four times the plan, which really does drive churn. SHAP splits each prediction fairly among the features, using game theory. This customer starts from the average, point five oh. The monthly plan adds point one eight, fifty-six months of tenure takes away point two two, and the other two bring it to point four seven five. Averaged over customers, SHAP ranks the plan first and the I D last.",
